Considering the excellent SPR of HKUST- $\textcircled{a}\mathrm{PS}_{-}63$ , we also studied its $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ sorption capacity. Type I $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ isotherms were collected at $295\mathrm{~K~}$ and 0.96 bar on HKUST-1 and on $\mathrm{HKUST}{\cdot}1@\mathrm{PS}\_63$ . The former absorbed $93.9\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $16.5\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ and the latter 61.6 of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $13.0~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (expressed in $\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ per grams
The aforementioned result prompted us to compare the long-term hydrolytic stability of nonencapsulated HKUST-1 crystals to that of HKUST- $1@\mathrm{PS}_{-}63$ , by exposing each one to high relative humidity (RH). The two materials were placed in an incubation chamber at $80\%$ RH and $300\mathrm{~K~}$ (Figure S8, Supporting Information). Samples of each material were taken at different time-points during incubation, and then studied by $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ isotherms and by XRPD (Figure 3d and Figures S9 and S10, Supporting Information). The $S_{\mathrm{BET}}$ of HKUST-1 decreased by $56\%$ after $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ incubation, by $78\%$ after $24\mathrm{h}$ , and by $96\%$ after 1 week (final value: $64.0\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . In contrast, the composite was much more resistant to the same conditions: its $S_{\mathrm{BET}}$ (expressed in grams of HKUST-1) decreased from 1202 to $1111\ensuremath{\mathrm{~m~}^{2}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{~g}^{-1}}$ after $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ ( $\mathrm{^{\prime}S P R}=77\%$ ; relative to the initial HKUST-1 crystals – $S_{\mathrm{BET}}\mathrm{:}$ $1430\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ , whereas after one week its $S_{\mathrm{BET}}$ decreased to $782~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ $\mathrm{SPR}=55\%$ . Interestingly, even after 1 month of incubation, the composite retained some of its initial sorption capacity, showing a $S_{\mathrm{BET}}$ of $417{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}$ $(\mathrm{SPR}=29\%$ ).
